in this lesson we're going to talk about  absolute value so this is something that  they test on the act  and the important thing to know about  absolute value is that you want to treat  the absolute value bars just like you  would print parentheses right you want  to do that part first  what's inside and then whatever you get  for your answer  the final result is going to be positive  so in this case you can see the absolute  value of negative three is going to be  positive three the absolute value of  positive three will also be positive  three so you don't wanna you don't wanna  think that it's the opposite you just  want to think do the operation inside  the absolute value bars  the final result is going to be positive  okay  here's a common mistake that sometimes  students will make they'll see like you  know the absolute value of 2x minus 3  and they'll say oh i have to make this  positive i'm going to make this plus 3.  you don't want to make that mistake you  just want to do the operation here first  and then  your final answer will be a positive  value so let's do a couple examples here  you can pause the video and try these on  your own and we'll go through them so  this first one i'm going to just work  from the inside out right so 4 times  negative 2 is a negative 8.  okay  we've got a negative 5 times a negative  1 is actually a positive 5. a negative 8  plus 5 is negative 3  okay and then the absolute value of  negative 3 is positive 3.  okay now in this example the absolute  value of negative 2 is positive 2 okay  multiply by negative 3.  the absolute value of positive 2 is 2  times 4 is 8  negative 3 times 2 is negative 6  and negative 6 plus 8 is positive 2. so  you can see i'm basically following the  order of operations i'm treating the  absolute values like parentheses doing  that operation  there's no exponents we go to  multiplication division then lastly  addition subtraction so this is a brief  review of just how to work with absolute  values i'll see you in the next lesson
 

